  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\n- Small chances of rain mainly North each night through Fri night\n\nNot much change expected in the upper air pattern for the region as \ncompared to previous forecasts. Rain chances have increased just \nslightly for each night through Friday night. \n\nThe northern branch of the upper jet core is expected to remain just \nnorth of the area through Thursday night. It is far enough north to \nkeep the forecast area out of diurnal chances of rain and more \nwidespread cloud cover. However, it is just close enough that we \ncontinue to get barely clipped by short wave troughs embedded in the \nflow.\n\nFor tonight, Wednesday night, and Thursday night, the timing of the \nshort waves are such that they are bringing the very limited rain \nchances each night. This limits the amount of instability helped by \nthe diurnal boost. In addition, these waves have no access to any \nlow level moisture below 7-8k ft. This will limit any rain to mainly \nsprinkles, to just isolated coverage of any showers that would \nproduce measurable rain.\n\nThe upper jet nearby will finally lift northward after Thursday \nnight as the long wave trough is able to move out. The building \nridge will help a sfc warm front lift NNE toward the area Friday \nnight. This is likely to be another round of sprinkles and a few \nlight showers with limited low level moisture once again.\n\n- Warming temperatures this weekend\n\nThe warm front will move away from the area by Saturday morning, \nallowing the warm sector to move up and over the area for Saturday. \nThe models have been fairly consistent in bringing +20C air over the \narea at 850 mb. This would translate to max temperatures at the sfc \napproaching 80F. Plenty of subsidence over the area on Saturday will \nprotect the area from any rain through Saturday night.\n\n- Rain chances return Sunday for southern areas\n\nWe have been mentioning a system that will be over the Gulf late in \nthe week that will try to approach the area later Sunday. This \nsystem has some potential tropical moisture as it will be over the \nGulf to start with. The long wave flow will be arranging itself on \nthe backside of the ridge to be oriented from SW to NE. This flow, \nand a short wave that is embedded with the upper flow will will be \noriented from SW to NE, and will try to absorb the tropical system \nand drive it toward the region. The trend for this system has been \nfor it to inch toward the area as it rotates out of the Western Gulf.\n\nUpper lows are notorious for wobbling and not following the forecast \ntrack very well. This one would be becoming extra-tropical as it \nleaves the Gulf coast region. We will need to monitor the movement \nof this system as if it can rotate a bit further north, it will \nbring more rain chances on Sunday than we have been advertising.\n\nOnce this low rotates away from the area later Sunday, we will fall \nunder back under upper ridging, dry out, and warm back up.",
